ZERO OS 0.7.1 ANIME COMMAND VERIFICATION
Date: 2026-11-16 UTC

Tested ISO SHA256:
4b690138dfd0e3bc759d1a4163f3c73c528977f950bb62cbf7919204a6c4a1e9
build/zero.iso remained unchanged throughout all five runs.

QEMU TCG graphical boot modes, no data disks attached:
BIOS x86-64: PASS, 13 terminal commands.
BIOS x86-32 with long mode and PAE disabled: PASS, 13 commands.
UEFI x86-64: PASS, 13 commands.
UEFI x86-32 with long mode disabled: PASS, 13 commands.

In each graphical mode, PS/2 keyboard input exercised the actual shell:
- Disable animation, hide the portrait, then anime shows it.
- Repeating anime keeps the portrait visible.
- Hide again and pause graphics; anime extra is rejected.
- tasks confirms the invalid command leaves graphics paused.
- anime resumes graphics and restores the portrait.
- help contains anime; clear followed by anime remains usable.

Seven actual VM screenshots per graphical mode were captured. Exact RGB
comparisons of the entire 288x384 portrait region prove:
- shown differs from hidden;
- shown equals repeated;
- hidden equals hidden-again;
- hidden-again equals invalid-unchanged;
- shown equals resumed and final.
The BIOS64 and UEFI32 final screenshots were also visually inspected.

No-framebuffer IA32 run: PASS, 3 commands via the actual keyboard path.
anime returns No supported linear framebuffer.; anime extra is rejected;
tasks remains available and computation continues. This configuration used
ISA VGA without an available default VGA ROM, so it is a serial-observed
no-framebuffer case. Its screenshot reports that the display is not
initialized; this is not evidence of a rendered text console.
The first attempt had an unsupported host-side isa-vga.romfile property;
removing that property fixed VM startup. No guest changes were required.

All five VM processes exited. No existing user data disks were attached or
modified. Test infrastructure uses QEMU; native VirtualBox was not run.
run.sh reproduces the focused tests; optional arguments select boot modes.
session.serial and command-N.txt files preserve actual terminal output.
The script also writes private PPM/pixel/firmware-variable artifacts for
verification; these are not needed in the distributed ISO/source archive.
